.SH DESCRIPTION
.B drivel
mounts a local directory as a loopback FUSE filesystem that acts as an
.IR interceptor :
every operation is proxied to an underlying backing directory (the source of
truth / local cache) and, asynchronously and bidirectionally, synced with a
cloud-storage provider. The first and currently only provider is
.BR "Google Drive" ,
driven by the Drive API v3
.I changes.list
cursor feed \(em not webhooks and not the Workspace Events API.
.PP
Filesystem operations never block on the network: reads, lookups, and attribute
calls pass straight through to the backing directory, while each mutation is
emitted as a change event and handled off the FUSE path by the sync engine.
Inbound changes from the provider are pulled into the backing directory by a
cursor-driven poll loop, with echo/loop suppression so a change that originated
locally is not re-applied when it echoes back from the cloud.
.PP
.B drivel
is a client you run yourself. It ships no application identity and collects no
analytics or telemetry; you supply your own Google Cloud OAuth credentials, so
all Drive traffic is directly between your machine and Google under an app
identity you control. All Drive traffic uses HTTP/3 (QUIC) with an automatic
fallback to HTTP/2.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B login
Interactive, rclone-style OAuth wizard. Collects the client ID/secret (from
flags, an existing
.IR credentials.json ,
or prompts), writes
.IR credentials.json ,
runs the loopback/paste login flow, and caches the resulting token to
.IR token.json .
This is the only interactive subcommand.
.TP
.B mount
Mount a directory and sync it. Non-interactive: it requires a token produced by
.B login
and never prompts on stdin. This is the default command, so
.B drivel -mount \fIdir\fB ...
(with a leading flag and no subcommand word) also selects it.
.TP
.B help
Print top-level usage.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -config " file"
TOML file describing one or more mounts; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
When neither
.B -config
nor
.B -mount
is given,
.B drivel
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/config.toml
if it exists, so
.B "drivel mount"
on its own serves everything configured.
.IP
It cannot be combined with any flag that describes
.I what
to mount \(em that would require a precedence rule between the two \(em though
.B -debug
and
.B -pprof
compose with it.
.TP
.BI -mount " dir"
Path to mount the filesystem. Required unless
.B -config
is used. Created if missing.
.TP
.BI -data " dir"
Backing directory used as the source of truth. If omitted,
.B drivel
uses
.I in-place mode
(Linux only): the mountpoint is its own backing store, so files remain in it
after
.B drivel
exits. See
.BR "IN-PLACE MODE" .
.TP
.BI -credentials " file"
OAuth client-secret JSON. Supplying it enables Drive sync; without it,
.B drivel
runs in log-only mode (no network, no auth) and merely reports the change events
it would sync.
.TP
.BI -token " file"
Path to the cached OAuth token written by
.BR "drivel login" .
Default:
.IR token.json .
.TP
.BI -state " file"
Path to the sync-state database (the change-feed cursor and echo-suppression
records). Kept outside the backing tree so it is not itself synced. Default:
.IR drivel-state.db .
.TP
.BI -index " file"
Path to the provider's path\(->ID index: a cache that lets a restart resolve
remote objects without re-deriving every mapping from the API. It is never
authoritative \(em entries are verified against the remote before they are used,
and anything missing is looked up again \(em so deleting this file costs API
round trips and nothing else. Kept outside the backing tree. An empty value
disables it. Default:
.IR drivel-index.db .
.TP
.BI -drive-root " id"
Drive folder ID mapped to the mount root. Use
.B root
for the My Drive root. Default:
.BR root .
.TP
.BI -drive-sweep-mode " mode"
How the enumeration sweep walks Drive.
.B scoped
descends from
.B -drive-root
one directory at a time, so the cost is proportional to the folder mounted.
.B flat
lists the whole account, one request per thousand objects, and filters afterwards.
.B auto
(the default) descends unless
.B -drive-root
names the whole Drive, where there is no subtree to save.
.IP
A descent spends roughly one request per directory, where a flat listing spends one
per thousand objects in the account, so a subtree holding a very large number of
near-empty directories can be cheaper to sweep
.BR flat .
Drivel logs when a descent looks folder-dense rather than leaving it to be
discovered.
.TP
.B -lazy
Enable lazy hydration. Remote files are materialised in the backing directory as
.IR placeholders :
they carry the real name, size and modification time but occupy no space, and
their content is fetched the first time something reads (or partially writes)
them. A directory listing therefore costs no downloads. Requires
.BR -credentials ,
since a placeholder is a promise that the content can be fetched later.
.IP
Without this flag the backing directory holds full content, which is the default.
.IP
Placeholder state is recorded in an extended attribute on each backing file
.RB ( user.drivel.placeholder
on Linux and macOS;
.B drivel.placeholder
in
.B EXTATTR_NAMESPACE_USER
on FreeBSD), which is what stops the sync engine from uploading a placeholder's zero
bytes over the real remote file. It is the only such record: if the backing
filesystem cannot store user extended attributes,
.B drivel
warns at startup and lazy mode is unsafe there, because an unmarked placeholder is
indistinguishable from an empty file. Use eager mode or a different
.I -data
directory; no database stands in for the attribute.
.TP
.B -xattr
Serve extended attributes through the mountpoint by passing them to the backing
store. Off by default: without it,
.B drivel
answers every xattr operation on the mount with
.BR EOPNOTSUPP ,
as a filesystem with no xattr support does.
.IP
The default is a safety property, not a performance one. A
.B drivel
mount is an interceptor rather than a plain loopback, and its own control metadata
lives in a
.B user.drivel.placeholder
attribute on the backing file (see
.BR -lazy ).
Passing attributes through publishes that marker at the mountpoint and makes it
writable by anything that can write there: stripping it from an unhydrated
placeholder makes the next upload replace the remote file with the placeholder's
zero bytes, and attaching it to an ordinary file makes the next read fetch the
remote copy over local content. Neither requires privilege and neither is
reported.
.IP
Nothing inside
.B drivel
needs the passthrough (em the hydrator reads and writes its marker on the backing
file directly, below the mount (em so enabling it is for applications on the
mountpoint that want extended attributes of their own. Extended attributes are
not synced to the provider in any case; see the design note on POSIX metadata in
DESIGN.md.
.TP
.B -resync
Enumerate the whole remote tree and reconcile it against the backing directory at
startup, even when a baseline already exists.
.IP
A sweep runs automatically without this flag in three cases: the first run (there
is no change cursor yet, so the remote tree has never been looked at), a sweep that
a previous run left unfinished, and a change cursor the provider says has expired.
It lists metadata only \(em roughly one request per 1000 objects, no content \(em
and runs in the background, so the mount comes up immediately and stays usable
while it proceeds.
.IP
The change-feed start token is taken
.I before
the sweep and adopted only after it finishes, so nothing that changes during the
sweep is missed.
.TP
.B -materialize
During a reconcile, create local copies of remote files that have none. Under
.B -lazy
this always happens and costs nothing (the copy is a placeholder), so the flag is
only meaningful in the default eager mode, where materialising means downloading
the entire remote tree \(em a decision the user makes rather than a side effect of
mounting.
.TP
.BI -max-deletes " n"
Cap on the deletions one reconcile may infer, in either direction. Default:
.BR 100 ;
.B 0
removes the limit.
.IP
Exceeding the cap abandons the delete pass entirely rather than trimming it. A
reconcile only ever infers a deletion for a path it has previously synced (never
from a file merely being absent on one side), so a large count almost always means
the premise is wrong rather than that there are that many real deletions \(em a
state database reused against a different
.BR -drive-root ,
a fresh empty backing directory, a mount pointing somewhere new. Note that removing
a remote object uses the provider's delete, which on Drive is permanent rather than
a move to the trash.
.IP
A refused pass still counts as a completed sweep, so raising the cap and restarting
does not retry it. If the deletions are genuine, re-run with
.B -resync
as well \(em otherwise nothing happens until the next
.B -sweep-interval
falls due.
.TP
.BI -sweep-interval " d"
Re-enumerate and reconcile the remote tree this often, timed from the last
.I completed
sweep rather than from process start. Default:
.BR 24h ;
.B 0
disables it.
.IP
The change feed only reports what happens while
.B drivel
is watching, so anything that happened while it was not running stays
unreconciled until something enumerates the tree again. Without a periodic sweep
that means a first run, a resumed sweep, an expired cursor or
.BR -resync ,
which for a long-lived mount can be never.
.TP
.BI -upload-workers " n"
How many files this mount uploads at once. Default:
.BR 4 .
Must be 1 or more; an explicit
.B 0
is refused rather than read as \(lquse the default\(rq.
.IP
Writes to the same path are serialised whatever this is set to, because concurrent
uploads of one file can land out of order; the concurrency is across files. Raising
it buys less than it appears to: every push shares one HTTP/3 connection and so one
congestion window, meaning extra workers overlap the per-file round trips rather
than moving more bytes. Each in-flight upload can also hold a chunk buffer \(em
16 MiB on Drive \(em and past the point the provider begins throttling, more
workers cost quota and gain nothing.
.TP
.BI -hydrate-workers " n"
How many placeholders this mount fetches at once under
.BR -lazy .
Default:
.BR 8 .
Must be 1 or more, on the same grounds as
.BR -upload-workers .
Ignored in eager mode, where nothing hydrates.
.IP
This is what bounds a recursive read over a lazy tree, which would otherwise fault
every file simultaneously. It is higher than the upload default because a typical
link's downlink is several times its uplink, and because it sits on the read path:
a caller is blocked on every fetch it governs, so
.B 1
turns a parallel read into a queue.
.IP
Both counts are per mount. A limit shared between mounts would let either one
starve the other and let each infer, from how long it waited, when the other was
busy; Drivel budgets nothing across mounts. Neither number governs the inbound
change feed, which applies remote changes one at a time and has no knob.
.TP
.B -debug
Enable FUSE debug logging.
.TP
.BI -pprof " addr"
Serve Go's
.B net/http/pprof
profiling endpoints on
.I addr
(for example
.BR localhost:6060 ,
or a bare
.B 6060
for the same thing), giving
.B go tool pprof
goroutine, heap, CPU and trace profiles of the running process. One endpoint
serves the whole process, not one per mount. Empty, the default, starts nothing.
.IP
This endpoint serves the process's heap \(em which for a filesystem holds the
paths, and in a buffer somewhere the contents, of the files being synced \(em to
anyone who can reach it, and lets them start a CPU profile. It therefore binds
only a loopback address: anything else is refused at startup unless
.B -pprof-allow-remote
is also given. Bind it to localhost and tunnel to it. A port that cannot be
bound is a startup error rather than a warning, so a run started in order to be
measured never silently produces nothing.
.IP
.B /debug/pprof/cmdline
is not served. It would return this process's command line, which names the
credentials file, the token, the backing directory and the account.
.TP
.B -pprof-allow-remote
Permit
.B -pprof
to bind an address that is not loopback, publishing the profiling endpoint \(em
and so the paths and some contents of synced files \(em to whoever can reach
that address. Refused on its own, without
.BR -pprof .
.SH LOGIN OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -account " name"
Store this login as a named account: its credentials and token go in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/name ,
created mode 0700, and
.BI [account. name ]
is appended to the config file.
.IP
Without it,
.B drivel login
behaves as it always has and writes to the current directory. With several
accounts a shared token file is not a limitation but a hazard: each login would
silently overwrite the previous one's credentials.
.TP
.BI -config " file"
Config file to add the account to. Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/config.toml .
Only ever appended to \(em an account that is already defined is printed for you
to reconcile by hand rather than rewritten, so comments and layout survive.
.TP
.BI -credentials " file"
Path to read/write the OAuth client-secret JSON. Default:
.IR credentials.json .
.TP
.BI -token " file"
Path to write the OAuth token. Default:
.IR token.json .
.TP
.BI -client-id " id"
OAuth client ID. If omitted, it is read from
.I -credentials
or prompted for.
.TP
.BI -client-secret " secret"
OAuth client secret. If omitted, it is read from
.I -credentials
or prompted for.
.TP
.BI -project-id " id"
GCP project ID (optional).
.TP
.BI -scope " name"
Drive scope: one of
.BR drive ", " drive.readonly ", or " drive.file .
If omitted, the wizard shows a menu.
.TP
.BI -port " n"
Loopback port for the OAuth redirect;
.B 0
picks a free port automatically. Default:
.B 53682
(rclone's port).
.TP
.B -open
Attempt to open the authorization URL with the operating system's browser
handler.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
The config file describes any number of mounts, which one
.B drivel
process serves together. A single mount needs nothing beyond its flags; the file
is what you graduate to when one stops being enough.
.PP
An
.I account
is a set of credentials \(em a provider kind plus that provider's settings. A
.I mount
references an account and says what to mount from it. Their provider settings are
merged, with the mount's winning, so one account can be mounted more than once
with different roots.
.PP
.RS
.EX
[account.personal]
provider    = "gdrive"
credentials = "/home/you/.config/drivel/personal/credentials.json"
token       = "/home/you/.config/drivel/personal/token.json"

[[mount]]
account = "personal"
path    = "~/drive-personal"
data    = "~/.cache/drivel/personal"
lazy    = true

[mount.provider]
root = "root"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount keys mirror the flags:
.IR name ,
.IR account ,
.IR path ,
.IR data ,
.IR state ,
.IR lazy ,
.IR xattr ,
.IR debug ,
.IR resync ,
.IR materialize ,
.I max-deletes
and
.IR sweep-interval .
An unrecognised key is an error rather than a silent no-op. Relative paths resolve
against the config file's own directory, so a config directory can be moved or
kept in a dotfiles repository. Within a
.B [mount.provider]
or account table, a value is treated as a path only when written like one \(em
beginning
.RI \(dq ~/ \(dq,
.RI \(dq ./ \(dq
or
.RI \(dq ../ \(dq
\(em since a folder ID is a string too.
.PP
Mounts are validated against each other before any of them is opened, because
several mounts can damage each other in ways one cannot. Two mounts may not share
a sync-state database (each engine would read the other's echo records as its own
baseline, which is also what reconcile infers deletions from), a mountpoint, or a
name; a backing directory may not sit inside another mount's mountpoint, or its
reads would be routed back through
.BR drivel ;
and a state database may not sit inside any backing tree, or it would sync itself
to the cloud and its own writes would generate the events causing more.
.SH IN-PLACE MODE
Omitting
.I -data
selects in-place mode, in which the mount directory is its own backing store.
.B drivel
opens a directory handle to the mountpoint
.I before
mounting over it and routes all backing I/O through
.RI ( /proc/self/fd/N )
so it bypasses the FUSE overlay instead of recursing into it. Nothing is copied,
and the files remain in place after unmount. In-place mode is Linux-only; on
other platforms supply a separate
.I -data
directory.
.SH SPECIAL FILES AND MOUNT SAFETY
.B drivel
always mounts
.B nodev
and
.BR nosuid ,
and there is no option to disable them: a device node or a setuid binary arriving
from a remote is never something a user asked for. This protects the mountpoint
only \(em with
.I -data
the backing directory is an ordinary directory reachable without going through
.BR drivel ,
and nothing there is affected. On FreeBSD only
.B nosuid
is requested, because the kernel has no
.B nodev
flag and only
.B devfs
may hold device nodes.
.PP
.B Hard links are refused
with
.BR EPERM ,
the error
.BR link (2)
defines for a filesystem that cannot create them. A path\-addressed cloud store
cannot express two names sharing one copy of the bytes. Permitting the link would
leave two ordinary files that are both uploaded and diverge from each other on the
first write, having reported success.
.PP
.BR Symlinks ,
FIFOs, sockets and device nodes are created normally in the backing directory and
are never synced \(em they carry no byte stream, and no provider can represent
them.
.B drivel
logs one line naming each one, from the mount when it is created and from the
enumeration sweep when the local walk steps over it, so a file that is not syncing
says so rather than merely being absent elsewhere. On FreeBSD such a file cannot be
created through the mount at all: the attempt fails with
.B EINVAL
and creates nothing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT AND REQUIREMENTS
.B drivel
requires the FUSE mount helper
.RB ( fusermount3 ),
which ships with the system
.B fuse3
package (install with
.B "dnf install fuse3"
or
.BR "apt install fuse3" ).
HTTP/3/QUIC benefits from a larger UDP receive buffer; if
.B quic-go
warns about the buffer size, raise it with
.BR "sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=7500000" .
.SH PLATFORM SUPPORT
.B drivel
is built and tested on
.BR Linux ,
on every architecture Go supports, and on
.B FreeBSD
15.1, where the full test suite has been run with nothing skipped. It also
compiles for
.B macOS
(amd64 and arm64), but that build is
.I unverified
\(em it has never been run against a live Drive. Treat it accordingly.
.B Windows
is not supported and is not planned; use WSL2, or Google's native Drive client.
Other Unixes (OpenBSD, NetBSD, Solaris, illumos, AIX) lack a Go FUSE binding.
.PP
Two limitations apply off Linux.
.TP
.B In-place mode is unavailable
It routes backing I/O through
.RI ( /proc/self/fd/N ),
and only Linux has procfs. Supply
.I -data
on other platforms.
.TP
.B -lazy is untested on macOS
Lazy hydration marks undownloaded files with an extended attribute, which is what
prevents an empty placeholder being uploaded over live remote content.
.B drivel
implements extended attributes on all three platforms, and FreeBSD has been
verified, but the code has never been run on a Mac: treat lazy mode there as
untested and prefer the default eager mode.
.TP
.B -lazy is unsafe without extended attributes, on any platform
If the marker cannot be written there is no record that a file is a placeholder at
all, and an unfetched file is indistinguishable from an empty one. This covers a
.I -data
directory on
.I /mnt/c
or another drvfs mount under WSL2, on exFAT or FAT, and on a FreeBSD
.I tmpfs
(UFS and ZFS carry extended attributes;
.I tmpfs
does not).
.B drivel
warns at startup, naming the directory: the fix is eager mode or a different
.IR -data ,
not preserving any database.
.TP
.B macOS backing directories belong on APFS or HFS+
On exFAT, FAT and some network volumes macOS keeps extended attributes in hidden
.I ._name
companion files rather than in the filesystem, which would place the placeholder
marker inside the synced tree.
.B drivel
detects this and reports the store as having no extended attributes.
.PP
On macOS,
.B macFUSE
is the only supported FUSE implementation:
.B drivel
looks for
.I mount_macfuse
or
.I mount_osxfuse
and nothing else, so
.B FUSE-T
does not work. macFUSE 4.x is not open source and its own terms restrict commercial
use;
.B drivel
neither bundles nor distributes it, and its AGPLv3 licence is unaffected by your use
of it \(em the two are separate programs communicating over a file descriptor, not a
combined work.
.SH SIGNALS
.B drivel mount
unmounts cleanly on
.B SIGINT
.RB ( Ctrl-C )
or
.BR SIGTERM :
the mount is unmounted first (flushing pending FUSE events), then the sync engine
drains its bounded queue of in-flight uploads before the process exits. A second
.B Ctrl-C
forces an immediate exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/config.toml
Mount and account configuration; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
Read when
.B drivel mount
is given neither
.B -config
nor
.BR -mount .
Defaults to
.I ~/.config/drivel/config.toml
when
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/drivel/<account>/
Per-account credentials and token, created mode 0700 by
.BR "drivel login -account" .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/drivel/<name>/state.db
Default sync-state database for a configured mount, one per mount. Defaults under
.I ~/.local/state
when
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I credentials.json
OAuth client secret (client ID/secret). Treat as a secret; do not commit.
.TP
.I token.json
Cached OAuth token from
.BR "drivel login" .
.TP
.I drivel-state.db
bbolt sync-state database (change-feed cursor + echo records).
.TP
.I drivel-index.db
bbolt path\(->ID index for the provider. A cache; safe to delete.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Authenticate:
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel login
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount with a separate backing directory (log-only, no cloud):
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel mount -mount ./mnt -data ./data
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount in-place on Linux (the directory is its own backing store):
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel mount -mount ./dir
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mount with Google Drive sync:
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel mount -mount ./mnt -data ./data \e
    -credentials credentials.json -token token.json -drive-root <folderID>
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set up two accounts and serve both from one process:
.PP
.RS
.EX
drivel login \-account personal
drivel login \-account work
$EDITOR ~/.config/drivel/config.toml   # add a [[mount]] for each
drivel mount
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B drivel
exits
.B 0
on a clean unmount,
.B 2
on an unknown command or flag-parse error, and non-zero on a fatal mount, auth,
or sync-setup error.
.SH SEE ALSO
